// 01 · the graph — one graph, two operations
//
// A Mentl program is not text that a compiler parses into some separate
// internal representation. It is one graph. Every name, every value, every
// type is a node with a handle (an Int), and there are exactly two operations
// on it: inference draws an edge (the one writer), and the cursor projects a
// read. There is no symbol table beside the graph and no second IR beneath
// it — the graph IS the symbol table, and it holds the type of every name at
// every point.
//
// That is why "what is the type of x here?" is never a guess. The medium does
// not re-derive it; it follows the edge inference already drew and reads the
// live node. When you point the cursor at `add` below, the type you see is
// the type the graph proved, not a fresh re-analysis.
//
// Run it:   mentl run  lib/tutorial/01-graph.mn
// Read it:  mentl lib/tutorial/01-graph.mn:24:4   ← the cursor on add; its inferred type
import io

// add — the smallest binding. Inference writes several edges here: a node for
// `add` itself (typed `(Int, Int) -> Int`), one for `a`, one for `b`, one for
// the body's result unified with the return. Its effect row comes out `Pure`
// because the body performs nothing — the graph proves that; you did not say it.
fn add(a, b) = a + b

// triple closes over `add`. Two reads resolve it: the environment walk finds
// the name in scope, and the graph chase finds its type. One surface, two
// projections of the same graph — which is why cross-scope reference needs no
// special case.
fn triple(x) = {
  let doubled = add(x, x)
  let tripled = add(doubled, x)
  tripled
}

// The same graph holds this module's bindings alongside the imported ones:
// `int_to_str` and `print_string` come from io, and composing with
// them is the same edge-following as composing with `add`. The graph spans
// the whole program.
fn main() = triple(7) |> int_to_str |> print_string
